      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, my tomato plants are acting weird. the leaves on the bottom of the stems are turning this sickly yellow color but the top ones look okay. what's probably going on and how do i fix the soil?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The yellowing leaves on the lower stems of your tomato plants, a classic case of \"gollum's favorite leaf\" - the lower leaves are being drained of their nutrients, while the top ones are still getting the good stuff. It's a common issue, especially when the plant is producing fruit, as it's diverting energy from the lower leaves to the fruiting branches. You can try pruning the lower leaves to direct the plant's energy towards the top, where the sunlight is still plentiful. It's a bit like a wise old goblin, knowing when to let go of the old to make way for the new.\n\nAs for the soil, it's possible that the lower leaves are being deprived of oxygen, which can cause the yellowing. Check the soil moisture and pH levels, and consider adding some organic matter like compost or well-rotted manure to improve the soil's structure and fertility. A good rule of thumb is to add a 2-inch layer of compost to the soil, and mix it in well. It's a bit like a goblin's treasure trove, where the good stuff is hidden beneath the surface, waiting to be uncovered.\n\nAlso, keep an eye out for pests like aphids or white",
